      {
        "id": 265399,
        "name": "",
        "username": "fjur",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/fjur/{size}/4849_2.png",
        "created_at": "2026-04-28T05:33:11.588Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"FrivYeti\" data-post=\"30\" data-topic=\"7735\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/frivyeti/48/1919_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> FrivYeti:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>why it was removed from Definitive Edition entirely.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Except, I’m not so sure it was. Rather, I think Savage Mana was actually substantially reworked into Definitive Haka’s Taonga Mana card. Indeed, neither the effect nor the name is the same, but both names share the word <em>mana</em> and the new card’s effect is somewhat reminiscent of the old—it still represents Haka drawing strength from victory, but now fits with him better both mechanically (it synergises with his card drawing and discarding mechanics) and narratively (he’s now longer killing people and absorbing their death energy and then doing a death shout), in my view. Plus, it removes a bunch of wonky interactions where he would, e.g. tuck the entire Organization under the card.</p>",
